WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:05.950
Some might argue that 2011's "The Thing" didn't
copy the 1982 original as well as the titular

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:12.240
monster does its victims, but fans seem to
disagree, judging by its popularity on Netflix.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.270
Though some movies may not fair well during
their tenure as a theatrical release, there

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:21.000
is always a chance for a movie to gain new
viewership when it comes to streaming services.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.859
Coughing up the amount of money required to
see a film typically makes some audiences

00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:29.449
apprehensive, and it seems like the only movies
that make money these days are Avengers-level outings.

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:34.579
Luckily, Netflix is there to help gain some
new viewership for movies that may not have

00:00:34.579 --> 00:00:37.390
been seen by a whole lot of people in theaters.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:39.950
This is the case for 2011's "The Thing."

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:44.060
Open up the movie catalog on Netflix, and
"The Thing" is not just the first film many

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:48.880
viewers will see advertised, but it is also
the third most popular movie on Netflix at

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.130
this moment.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:56.070
As a prequel/remake of 1982's "The Thing,"
the 2011 movie features the familiar and terrifying

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:59.989
story of an isolated research center located
in Antarctica.

00:00:59.989 --> 00:01:05.470
Starring Joel Edgerton, Mary Elizabeth Winstead,
Adewale Akinnuoye-Agbaje, and Ulrich Thomsen,

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:10.010
"The Thing" seemed like it would be an absolute
slam dunk as a new iteration of the beloved

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:11.450
science-fiction classic.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:16.780
Unfortunately, the 2011 version failed to
compel theatrical audiences, and some consider

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:20.060
it one of the worst sci-fi remakes of all
time.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.471
"Burn it!"

00:01:24.683 --> 00:01:28.130
"The Thing" tells the story of a far-flung
research settlement and its assortment of

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:32.720
scientists who soon find themselves under
attack by a shape-changing alien.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.520
This plot is similar to the original version
from 1982, and the paranoia that these individuals

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.530
feel often drives the plot forward as they
wonder who can they trust when there is an

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:47.729
extraterrestrial organism that can kill, assimilate,
and mimic other forms of life.

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:50.930
Audiences at the time didn't feel the need
to check out the movie, and the film missed

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:52.270
the mark at the box office.

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:57.320
"The Thing" was made for $38 million, but
the movie failed to break even, generating

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:03.740
just shy of $17 million in the United States
and $10.5 million in international markets.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:08.009
Besides just failing to make money, "The Thing"
received a middling reception, with Rotten

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:14.130
Tomatoes showing that the movie has a 34%
critic score and a 42% audience score.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:18.500
Despite a lackluster reception, some critics
saw potential in the 2011 movie.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.250
Keith &amp; the Movies wrote,

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:25.010
"Fans of the 1982 classic will want to see
it and should find a lot to like.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:28.650
While it trips itself up with an overloaded
cast and a few scenes which feel like they

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:34.569
belong in another film, it does deliver that
almost old-school sci-fi monster movie feel."

00:02:34.569 --> 00:02:39.170
Although 2011's "The Thing" failed to garner
a lot of attention during its original run,

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.280
it's dominating Netflix at the moment.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:45.650
Perhaps audiences were simply waiting for
an opportune time to watch the movie as the

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.990
original "The Thing" is considered a classic.

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:52.211
Now that 2011's version is just a click away,
it seems like viewers are giving the movie

00:02:52.211 --> 00:02:56.890
another chance, even though there are major
changes from the original source material.

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:01.240
Director Matthijs van Heijningen Jr. spoke
with Den of Geek in 2012 about some of the

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:05.690
changes and ideas he had while approaching
2011's "The Thing," which included how he

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:09.910
helped rewrite the first version of the script
and had Kate Lloyd as the main character as

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:12.281
opposed to the original's R.J. Macready.

00:03:12.281 --> 00:03:17.221
Heijningen added that the cast didn't need
much inspiration when taking on their respective roles.

00:03:17.221 --> 00:03:18.720
He said,

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.980
"Everyone who was part of this saw and loved
John Carpenter's movie.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:26.040
I didn't have to explain anything about that
sense of awkwardness because there was a great

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.970
example of it in the movie.

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:30.459
We saw the movie all together before we started.

00:03:30.459 --> 00:03:34.450
So it all felt very natural — it was already
familiar to everybody."

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:38.610
The similarities to the original film, as
well as the difference, seem to finally have

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:40.470
been embraced thanks to Netflix.

